1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,584
Welcome to Golden Hour — Hollywood, Then and Now.

2
00:00:03,584 --> 00:00:05,974
Some faces never really leave us.

3
00:00:05,974 --> 00:00:13,541
You hear one song, one laugh, one famous line, and a whole afternoon of your childhood comes rushing back.

4
00:00:13,541 --> 00:00:23,497
Tonight: fifty of the brightest stars of the sixties, seventies and eighties — and every single one of them is still with us in 2026.

5
00:00:23,497 --> 00:00:27,082
So keep count of how many you still remember.

6
00:00:27,082 --> 00:00:36,640
And stay to the very end — because the last face on our list outlived the man who turned her life into a thriller.

7
00:00:40,240 --> 00:00:46,102
Act One: Those We Never Forgot — the movie stars who owned the big screen.

8
00:00:46,102 --> 00:00:55,480
And we begin with a man who, in December of 2025, did something only a handful of performers in Hollywood history have ever done.

9
00:00:56,080 --> 00:01:03,991
Put him in a chimney-sweep's cap and a cockney grin, and Mary Poppins plays in your head all over again.

10
00:01:03,991 --> 00:01:12,298
But Bert was only the beginning — five decades of television followed, from The Dick Van Dyke Show to Diagnosis: Murder.

11
00:01:12,298 --> 00:01:17,044
At 98, he became the oldest performer ever to win an Emmy.

12
00:01:17,044 --> 00:01:24,560
And in December 2025, Dick Van Dyke blew out one hundred candles — still dancing, still grinning, still ours.

13
00:01:25,760 --> 00:01:31,477
You may remember the woman holding Cary Grant's hand on the train in North by Northwest.

14
00:01:31,477 --> 00:01:35,764
But the Oscar came earlier — On the Waterfront, 1954, opposite Brando.

15
00:01:35,764 --> 00:01:40,409
She never played the starlet; she played the woman who knew too much.

16
00:01:40,409 --> 00:01:49,342
Born on the Fourth of July, 1924, Eva Marie Saint is one hundred and two years old — the oldest living Oscar winner on Earth.

17
00:01:49,342 --> 00:01:52,200
The Academy's living legend, still writing her story.

18
00:01:53,400 --> 00:02:02,356
A cigar, a squint, three dollar bills — the Man with No Name didn't need dialogue to own the screen.

19
00:02:02,356 --> 00:02:11,759
Dirty Harry made him an icon; the director's chair made him immortal — four Oscars, from Unforgiven to Million Dollar Baby.

20
00:02:11,759 --> 00:02:14,446
Clint Eastwood turned 96 in May.

21
00:02:14,446 --> 00:02:18,476
His son says the Old Man has finally retired.

22
00:02:18,476 --> 00:02:25,640
After seventy years in front of and behind the camera — he's earned every silent sunset.

23
00:02:26,840 --> 00:02:33,229
One look from her, and you understood why the whole world fell for Italian cinema.

24
00:02:33,229 --> 00:02:46,006
She was a war child from the slums of Naples — and in 1961, Two Women made her the first actress ever to win an Oscar for a foreign-language performance.

25
00:02:46,006 --> 00:02:57,080
At 91, Sophia Loren remains the last great lady of Europe's golden screen — crowned in 2020 by one final, unforgettable performance in The Life Ahead.

26
00:02:58,280 --> 00:03:05,543
Just one octave of Supercalifragilistic, or one hilltop of Salzburg, and you can hear her instantly.

27
00:03:05,543 --> 00:03:11,443
Then, in 1997, the voice that defined two generations was silenced by surgery.

28
00:03:11,443 --> 00:03:21,430
Hollywood braced for tragedy — instead, she built a second act: The Princess Diaries, and the voice of Lady Whistledown narrating Bridgerton.

29
00:03:21,430 --> 00:03:29,600
Dame Julie Andrews turned 90 last October — proof that even when the singing stops, the music doesn't.

30
00:03:30,800 --> 00:03:37,479
In 1961, Anita danced through West Side Story like a flame — and took home the Oscar.

31
00:03:37,479 --> 00:03:49,658
Sixty years later, Steven Spielberg handed her a new role in the very same story — making her the only performer to appear in both versions of a Best Picture winner.

32
00:03:49,658 --> 00:03:59,480
Ninety-four years old, an EGOT before the term even existed, Rita Moreno is still onstage — because standing still was never part of her choreography.

33
00:04:00,680 --> 00:04:10,352
Barbarella in space, Bree Daniels on the phone — in the seventies, Jane Fonda was the most fearless woman on screen.

34
00:04:10,352 --> 00:04:21,866
Two Oscars later — Klute, Coming Home — she traded the screen for protest lines, and paid for it: the lists, the arrests, the nickname.

35
00:04:21,866 --> 00:04:28,775
At 88, she is still marching — climate protests, bare-knuckle activism, Grace and Frankie reruns.

36
00:04:28,775 --> 00:04:31,078
Hollywood trained her to perform.

37
00:04:31,078 --> 00:04:32,920
She chose to fight.

38
00:04:34,120 --> 00:04:37,517
That grey suit at the foot of the stairs.

39
00:04:37,517 --> 00:04:44,688
Vertigo made Kim Novak the most mysterious blonde of the fifties — Madeleine and Judy, the same woman twice.

40
00:04:44,688 --> 00:04:54,502
But Hollywood was a world she chose to leave: painting, poetry, horses, and a quiet life in Oregon, far from the spotlight that nearly broke her.

41
00:04:54,502 --> 00:04:59,031
At 93, Venice honored her with a Golden Lion for lifetime achievement.

42
00:04:59,031 --> 00:05:03,560
She came back on her own terms — and then went home.

